<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Hohnstein</h1><p class="st-meta">1182 CE – 1593 CE (approx.) · capital Honstein Castle</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">County of the southern Harz whose counts, named after Honstein castle, dominated the land around Nordhausen until their line died out in 1593.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The counts took their name from Honstein castle near Neustadt in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a> and appear as a distinct comital house in the later 12th century. They built up lordships around Klettenberg, Lohra and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goldene-aue">Golden Meadow</a>, surrounding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-nordhausen">Free Imperial City of Nordhausen</a>, and for a time also held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sondershausen">Sondershausen</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The county was a patchwork of castles, villages and forests on the southern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a>, often divided between branches of the family. The counts were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of greater neighbours, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halberstadt">Halberstadt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">Wettins</a>, and repeatedly pledged or sold lands to pay debts. Mining and forestry supplemented farming. Like much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, the county adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> in the 16th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last count, Ernst VII, died in 1593. His lands were divided among the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-brunswick">Duchy of Brunswick</a>-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-schwarzburg">Counts of Schwarzburg</a> and the Counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-stolberg">Stolberg</a>, and the Klettenberg and Lohra portions later passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> in 1648.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-hohnstein.jpg" alt="County of Hohnstein" width="320" height="222"><figcaption>Image: Norbert Radtke · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hohnstein1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1182 CE – 1593 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Honstein Castle</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ernst VII of Hohnstein</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, then Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Honstein</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1388&amp;focus=county-of-hohnstein" aria-label="County of Hohnstein on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4925 393 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Hohnstein lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5245 649L5229 694"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5245 649h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5245 649h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1388&amp;focus=county-of-hohnstein">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

